WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:07.220
Later in the specialization, we'll talk about debugging and diagnosing things that can go

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:09.500
wrong with learning algorithms.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:16.500
You also learn about specific tools to recognize when overfitting and underfitting may be occurring.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:20.380
But for now, when you think overfitting has occurred, let's talk about what you can

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:22.540
do to address it.

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:26.220
Let's say you fit a model and it has high variance.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:27.820
It's overfit.

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:32.260
This is an overfit house price prediction model.

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:37.080
One way to address this problem is to collect more training data.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.180
So that's one option.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:46.220
If you're able to get more data that is more training examples on sizes and prices of houses,

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:52.060
then with the larger training set, the learning algorithm will learn to fit a function that

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:54.140
is less wiggly.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:01:00.220
So you can continue to fit a high order polynomial or some other function with a lot of features.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:04.860
And if you have enough training examples, it will still do okay.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:10.780
So to summarize, the number one tool you can use against overfitting is to get more training

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:12.240
data.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:15.180
Now getting more data isn't always an option.

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:18.620
Maybe only so many houses have been sold in this location.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:21.740
So maybe there just isn't more data to be had.

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:24.780
But when the data is available, this can work really well.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:31.580
A second option for addressing overfitting is to see if you can use fewer features.

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:38.460
In the previous video, our model's features included the size x as was the size squared

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:44.420
and the six squared and xq and x4 and so on.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:47.980
These were a lot of polynomial features.

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:54.100
So in that case, one way to reduce overfitting is to just not use so many of these polynomial

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:55.500
features.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:58.380
But now let's look at a different example.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:02.420
Maybe you have a lot of different features of a house with which to try to predict its

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:07.740
price ranging from the size number of bedrooms, number of floors, the age, average income

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:13.260
of the neighborhood, and so on and so forth to the distance to the nearest coffee shop.

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:18.700
It turns out that if you have a lot of features like these, but don't have enough training

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:24.220
data, then your learning algorithm may also overfit to your training set.

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:29.180
Now instead of using all 100 features, if we were to pick just a subset of the most

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:37.100
useful ones, maybe size, bedrooms, and the age of the house, if you think those are

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:41.860
the most relevant features, then using just that smaller subset of features, you may

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:46.620
find that your model no longer overfits as badly.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:52.780
Choosing the most appropriate set of features to use is sometimes also called feature selection.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:57.780
One way you could do so is to use your intuition to choose what you think is the best set of

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:01.660
features, what's most relevant for predicting the price.

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:08.340
Now one disadvantage of feature selection is that by using only a subset of the features,

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:13.260
the algorithm is throwing away some of the information that you have about the houses.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:18.700
For example, maybe all of these features or 100 of them are actually useful for predicting

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:20.340
the price of a house.

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:24.660
So maybe you don't want to throw away some of the information by throwing away some of

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:26.660
the features.

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:32.340
Later in course two, you also see some algorithms for automatically choosing the most appropriate

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:35.660
set of features to use for prediction tasks.

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:39.740
Now this takes us to the third option for reducing overfitting.

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:44.500
This technique, which we'll look at in even greater depth in the next video, is called

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:47.200
regularization.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:54.460
If you look at an overfit model, here's a model using polynomial features, x x squared,

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:56.060
x cubed, and so on.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:04:00.300
You find that the parameters are often relatively large.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:05.580
Now if you were to eliminate some of these features, say if you were to eliminate the

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:13.120
feature x four, that corresponds to setting this parameter to zero.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:18.420
So setting a parameter to zero is equivalent to eliminating a feature, which is what we

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:21.060
saw on the previous slide.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:26.740
It turns out that regularization is a way to more gently reduce the impacts of some of

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:32.380
the features without doing something as harsh as eliminating it outright.

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:37.340
What regularization does is encourage the learning algorithm to shrink the values of

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:44.020
the parameters without necessarily demanding that the parameter is set to exactly zero.

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:49.460
And it turns out that even if you fit a higher order polynomial like this, so long as you

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:56.060
can get the algorithm to use smaller parameter values, w1, w2, w3, w4, you end up with

00:04:56.060 --> 00:05:00.860
a curve that ends up fitting the training data much better.

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:06.340
So what regularization does is it lets you keep all of your features, but it just prevents

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:12.300
the features from having an overly large effect, which is what sometimes can cause

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:13.300
overfitting.

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:21.140
By the way, by convention, we normally just reduce the size of the wj parameters that

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:23.420
is w1 through wn.

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:28.260
It kind of doesn't make a huge difference whether you regularize the parameter b as

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:29.260
well.

00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:31.540
You could do so if you want or not if you don't.

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:38.020
I usually don't, and it's just fine to regularize w1, w2, all the way to wn, but

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:41.700
not really encourage b to become smaller.

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:47.860
In practice, it should make very little difference whether you also regularize b or not.

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:49.620
So to recap.

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:54.460
These are the three ways you saw in this video for addressing overfitting.

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:55.780
1.

00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:57.260
Collect more data.

00:05:57.260 --> 00:06:02.540
If you can get more data, this can really help reduce overfitting.

00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:07.860
Sometimes that's not possible, in which case some of their options are 2.

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:12.180
Try selecting and using only a subset of the features.

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:16.620
You learn more about feature selection in course 2.

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:17.980
3.

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:23.740
Reduce the size of the parameters using regularization.

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:27.140
This will be the subject of the next video as well.

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:29.900
Just for myself, I use regularization all the time.

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:34.820
So this is a very useful technique for training learning algorithms, including neural networks

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:38.900
specifically which you see later in this specialization as well.

